/* ==============================================================
   07 — The singularity, and why lines cannot cross it
   --------------------------------------------------------------
   ROBOT     MCS500 only (R1)
   TEACHES   What a singularity is, the SCARA's one singularity,
             and the rules for moving at, near and through it
   NEEDS     Robot activated, NO TOOL, generous clearance
   WATCH     The elbow, not the tool tip. The whole phenomenon
             is visible at the elbow and nearly invisible at the
             tip
   TIME      ~60 s
   ============================================================== */

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   /!\ READ THIS BEFORE RUNNING

   Near a singularity, a slow-looking tool tip can need a FAST
   joint. That is the defining symptom, and it is the hazard: the
   tip creeps while the elbow whips.

   This file therefore caps joint speed with SetJointVelLimit
   rather than trusting SetCartLinVel. A Cartesian velocity limit
   does NOT protect you here — it limits the tip, and the tip is
   the thing that is moving slowly.

   Run it with the robot clear of everything, no tool fitted, and
   watch it the first time before you change any numbers.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   WHAT A SINGULARITY IS

   Not a maths problem. A physical blockage.

   Put the robot at its zero position — arm fully stretched — and
   try to move the end of the shaft straight OUT, further from
   the base. You cannot. Not "the controller refuses"; the arm
   has no more length to give.

   Now try to move it straight back IN, towards the base. That is
   possible, but not right away: from dead straight, the elbow's
   first few degrees of bend move the tip sideways, not inwards.
   To get any inward speed at all, the elbow has to bend fast.

   That is a singularity: a posture where the end of the arm
   loses the ability to move in some direction even though no
   joint is at its limit. It is a kind of workspace boundary.

   Mathematically, it is where the two joint-set solutions for a
   pose — lefty and righty — collapse into one. Which is the same
   thing said in a less useful way.

   ONE TYPE, NOT THREE

   A six-axis arm like the Meca500 has three kinds: wrist, elbow
   and shoulder. The MCS500 has ONE: the ELBOW singularity,

       θ2 = 0     the arm fully stretched

   whatever the other three joints are doing. Joints 3 and 4 —
   the shaft — can never be singular: they move straight up and
   down, and turn, and nothing they do depends on the arm.

   In space, it is a circle: wherever the axis of the shaft is
   225 mm from the axis of joint 1. The outer edge of the
   workspace drawing in the user manual, labelled as exactly
   that. Note that it is the SHAFT that matters, not the tool
   tip: with an off-axis tool, the tip can be anywhere when the
   arm is straight.

   Two facts make it impossible to ignore:

   1. THE ZERO POSITION IS ON IT. Every example in this repo
      starts and ends there. You have been visiting the
      singularity all along. (So does the MecaPortal's "Zero all
      joints" button — the user manual says so.)

   2. IT IS THE BORDER BETWEEN LEFTY AND RIGHTY. Joint 2 is
      positive in one, negative in the other, and zero on the
      line between. Every posture swap passes through it.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   THE RULES

   This is the part worth memorising, because the behaviour
   differs per command.

     MoveJoints   Always fine. You name joint positions, so no
                  inverse kinematics is solved and there is
                  nothing to be singular. Joint moves sail
                  straight through.

     MovePose     Fine. The manual is explicit: the robot can
                  transition through, or begin or end at, singular
                  postures without any issues. A MovePose that
                  swaps posture goes through it on the way.

     MoveLin*     Can START at the singularity, and can END at
                  it — the manual says the SCARA allows Cartesian
                  moves from or to a singular posture, which is
                  what makes jogging from the zero position work.
                  But it can NEVER CROSS it. Crossing means
                  swapping lefty for righty, and the manual is
                  blunt: it is physically impossible to follow a
                  linear path while changing configuration.

   That last rule is the big difference from the Meca500, whose
   straight lines can pass through two of its three
   singularities. On the SCARA, a straight line belongs to one
   posture from start to finish.

   AND THE WARNING THAT MATTERS MOST

   Close to the singularity, moving the tip in or out needs an
   elbow speed that grows without limit as the arm straightens.
   The robot keeps every joint within its limits by slowing the
   tip. So "nearly stretched" means "slow" — and the closer, the
   slower.

   The design rule that falls out of it: keep working points well
   inside the circle. A comfortable margin is the elbow bent by
   at least 30 degrees, which puts the shaft no more than about
   215 mm from the base axis. Use this file to understand the
   phenomenon so you can design paths that stay away from it.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */


/* --- Standard reset: start from a known state -----------------
   Deactivating loses every NON-persistent setting — tool and
   world frames, speeds, accelerations, blending, payload, torque
   limits, posture and turn selection, move mode, MoveJump
   settings — so this program starts from firmware defaults no
   matter what ran before it. Reactivating brings the motors
   back.

   This does NOT move the arm: the MCS500 has absolute encoders
   and never needs homing, which is why there is no Home() here.

   It does NOT reset persistent settings — joint limits, work
   zone, tool sphere, collision, P-Stop 2 and calibration config
   all survive. Run 00_common/00_cold_start.mxprog for those.

   This block is identical in every example. To remove it
   everywhere, see the README. */
DeactivateRobot()
ActivateRobot()
ResetError()
ResumeMotion()


/* --- Safety settings for this file ---------------------------- */

// The important one. Caps how fast any joint may turn, for EVERY
// Move* command. This is what protects you when the elbow has to
// hurry.
SetJointVelLimit(10)

SetJointVel(15)
SetCartLinVel(30)
SetBlending(0)          // see each arrival distinctly
SetAutoConf(1)


/* ============================================================
   DEMO 1 — starting AT the singularity
   ============================================================
   Go to the zero position, which is singular, and move straight
   back towards the base. */

MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 0)
Delay(1)

/* A straight line, 30 mm, straight in. Starting a linear move at
   the singularity is explicitly supported, so this is accepted
   rather than refused.

   Watch the START. The tip barely moves for a moment while the
   elbow breaks out of straight, then it picks up to the speed
   you asked for. That hesitation is the blocked direction from
   the top of this file, made visible.

   Which way does the elbow break — lefty or righty? From dead
   straight, both are equally good, and with automatic selection
   on it is the controller's choice. Worth knowing before you
   rely on it. */
MoveLin(195, 0, 109, 0)
Delay(1)

MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 0)
Delay(1)


/* ============================================================
   DEMO 2 — reaching for the edge
   ============================================================
   The same thing in reverse, and the one you meet in real
   programs: a straight line out towards the singularity,
   stopping just short of it. */

MovePose(170, 0, 100, 0)
Delay(1)

/* 54.5 mm straight out, ending half a millimetre inside the
   circle. Most of the way, the tip runs at the 30 mm/s asked
   for. Over the last several millimetres it visibly slows: the elbow
   is already at its speed cap, and every remaining millimetre
   needs more elbow than the last. Watch joint 2 — it is the one
   doing all the work. */
MoveLin(224.5, 0, 100, 0)
Delay(1)

MoveLin(170, 0, 100, 0)
Delay(1)


/* ============================================================
   DEMO 3 — the only way across is a joint move
   ============================================================
   Swap posture, and watch joint 2 on the way: it passes through
   zero. The arm straightens for an instant — on the singularity
   — and comes out the other side as its mirror image.

   This works because MovePose is a joint-space move. */

SetConf(1)              // righty
MovePose(170, 0, 100, 0)
Delay(1.5)

SetConf(-1)             // lefty — through the singularity
MovePose(170, 0, 100, 0)
Delay(1.5)


/* ============================================================
   DEMO 4 — the same swap, refused as a line
   ============================================================
   The robot is lefty now. Demand righty and ask for a short
   straight line.

   The robot refuses. The line would have to start lefty and end
   righty, and no straight line can do that: the posture can
   only change by passing through the singularity, which a line
   cannot cross. It declines the whole move rather than starting
   one it cannot finish. */

SetConf(1)

// Expect a refusal and an error here — [1033], the start
// position is not in the requested configuration. Nothing should
// move.
MoveLin(170, 40, 100, 0)

/* Reset the error before continuing: press the run button (now
   red), then resume. Or send ResetError() and ResumeMotion()
   from the editor. */
Delay(1)

/* Now hand the choice back and ask for exactly the same line.
   Accepted — and it stays lefty all the way, because a straight
   line cannot do anything else. The capability to change
   posture was never on offer; only the demand for it changed. */
SetAutoConf(1)
MoveLin(170, 40, 100, 0)
Delay(1)


/* --- Put everything back ------------------------------------- */

SetAutoConf(1)
SetJointVelLimit(100)
SetBlending(100)

MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 0)


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   A NOTE ON THE COORDINATES IN THIS FILE

   The singular condition here comes straight from the manual and
   is exact: θ2 = 0, the shaft 225 mm from the base axis, the
   zero position on it.

   The Cartesian start and end poses of the demos are
   constructed — lines towards, away from and alongside the
   singular circle, built to match the behaviour the manual
   describes rather than copied from it. They should behave as
   annotated, but exactly how slow the last millimetres of demo 2
   are depends on the speed caps, and which way the elbow breaks
   in demo 1 is the controller's choice. If a demo behaves
   differently from its comment, trust the robot and tell us:
   open an issue.

   THINGS TO TRY

   1. Demo 3 then demo 4, back to back. The joint-space swap
      works, the straight-line swap is refused, and the only
      difference is the kind of move. This is the clearest way to
      show what "a line cannot cross the singularity" means.

   2. In demo 2, change 224.5 to 220, then to 224.9. The first is
      unremarkable; the second crawls in. The penalty is not
      linear and it is not small.

   3. Set SetJointVelLimit(100) at the top and re-run demo 2 — at
      your own risk, with the robot clear. The elbow speeds that a
      near-singular line demands are the whole safety argument
      for this file, and on a SCARA they are quick.

   4. Put the robot at all-zeros and run SetAutoConf(0), then
      GetConf(). The controller has to freeze "the current
      posture" and cannot, because the arm is singular — so it
      picks righty, 1, as the manual says it will. A small thing,
      but it shows that "the current configuration" is not always
      a well-defined idea.

   NEXT   Nothing — this is the last file. Back to the README,
          and to DISCLAIMER.md for the list of places this repo
          simplifies, including this topic.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */
